---
import Callout from "../../guide/Callout.astro";
import GuidePage from "../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../guide/Path.astro";
import PlatformLabel from "../../guide/PlatformLabel.astro";
import Task from "../../guide/Task.astro";
import { plannedCapture } from "../../media/planned-captures.ts";
import { sitePath } from "../../site/links.ts";
---

<GuidePage
  manual="docs"
  slug="sync"
  lead="Sync keeps your library, reading progress, history and settings the same on your phone, tablet and computer, through a server you run yourself. Every device keeps its own full copy and works offline."
>
  <Task id="what-syncs" title="What syncs">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Synced</th> <th>Stays on each device</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Library, categories, read state and reading progress</td> <td>Downloads</td></tr>
        <tr><td>History and statistics</td> <td>Logins to sites</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Profiles and merged entries</td> <td>Screen and storage settings</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your settings, custom covers and fonts, extension repositories</td> <td>The device's own sync options</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each device chooses what it syncs under <Path>Settings › Sync › What syncs</Path>: reading history, statistics, categories, covers,
      fonts and other data, the settings of each Settings area, and which profiles. <strong>Library and reading progress</strong> always
      syncs. While the device syncs, Settings marks which settings sync, and the app bar of each Settings screen opens its area's list.
      The <strong>Library</strong> area also has a switch each for <strong>Library sort</strong>, <strong>Library filters</strong> and
      <strong>Chapter list defaults</strong>, which you set from menus rather than Settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each profile syncs as itself, so a family's profiles stay apart on every device. In a profile's menu under
      <Path>Settings › Profiles</Path>, <strong>Sync this profile</strong> turns it on or off for this device. A profile made on another
      device comes here on its own, unless <strong>Ask before a new profile comes here</strong> is on. Titles whose extension isn't
      installed on a device are listed under <strong>Install extensions for your titles</strong> on its Sync page. With
      <strong>Installed extensions</strong> on in What syncs, the Library, <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path> and that list also offer to
      install or uninstall the extensions you installed or uninstalled on your other devices.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sync needs a server. One person runs it, and everyone with an account on it can sync;
      <a href={sitePath("docs/sync-server/")}>Running a sync server</a> explains how.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task
    id="sign-in"
    title="Sign in the first device"
    shot={{ image: plannedCapture("sync-page.png"), alt: "The Sync page listing the account's devices", caption: "The Sync page" }}
  >
    <ol>
      <li>Open <Path>Settings › Sync</Path> and select <strong>Sign in with server address</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter the server's address, such as <code>https://sync.example.com</code>.</li>
      <li>
        Sign in with the username and password the server's admin gave you, or select <strong>I have an invite code</strong>, enter the code
        and choose your own.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The first sync uploads this device's library, or downloads the account's. Covers, fonts and the first download wait for Wi-Fi unless
      <strong>Use mobile data for covers, fonts and first download</strong> is on. Changes sync on any network unless
      <strong>Use mobile data for changes</strong> is off.
    </p>
    <p>
      When this device and the account both have data, <strong>Before the first sync</strong> shows how they come together: libraries, read
      state, history and progress are merged and nothing is deleted. Each of this device's profiles either pairs with one of the account's
      or is added as new; where only the name matches, choose <strong>Same as</strong> that profile or <strong>Add as new</strong>. For each
      paired profile, choose whether the account's settings or this device's settings are kept. Settings tied to the device, such as screen,
      storage and network ones, are never replaced.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Plain http://">
      <p>
        An <code>http://</code> address works only on your local network, and the address field says <strong>Not encrypted.</strong> From
        anywhere else, use the server's <code>https://</code> address.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      <PlatformLabel platform="Linux" lead />Katari keeps the sign-in in the desktop's keyring. Without one running, such as GNOME Keyring or
      KWallet, the Sync page asks you to start one.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task
    id="link"
    title="Link more devices"
    shot={{ image: plannedCapture("sync-link-code.png"), alt: "Link a device showing a code to scan", caption: "Link a device" }}
  >
    <ol>
      <li>On a device that already syncs, open <Path>Settings › Sync › Link a device</Path>. It shows a code that works for two minutes.</li>
      <li>
        On the new device, open <Path>Settings › Sync</Path> and select <strong>Scan a code from a linked device</strong>. Without a camera,
        select <strong>Enter a code from a linked device</strong> and type the server address and code instead.
      </li>
      <li>Confirm <strong>Join</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Signing in with your username and password on the new device works too.</p>
  </Task>

  <Task
    id="offline"
    title="Offline, and picking up on another device"
    shot={{
      image: plannedCapture("sync-handoff.png"),
      alt: "The reader offering to go where another device got to",
      caption: "Pick up where you stopped",
    }}
  >
    <p>
      Everything works without a connection. What you change is saved on the device and syncs when it can reach the server again; until then
      the Sync page says <strong>Offline · changes will sync when you're online</strong>. While Katari is closed, it syncs with
      <strong>Sync in the background</strong> on, every 30 minutes unless <strong>How often</strong> says otherwise. <strong>Pause syncing</strong>
      keeps changes on the device until you turn it off.
    </p>
    <p>
      When another device got further in a title, opening it in the reader or player offers to go there, such as
      <strong>Desktop is further</strong> over <strong>Chapter 12, page 18</strong>. Select <strong>Go there</strong> to jump, or dismiss it
      to stay.
    </p>
    <p>History names the device a title was last read on when it wasn't this one.</p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="questions" title="Needs your answer">
    <p>
      Most changes on two devices merge on their own. A few can't, so Katari keeps both and asks: the entry shows a banner, a notification
      says <strong>Open Sync to choose</strong>, and the Sync page offers <strong>Review and answer</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Migrated on two devices:</strong> a title was migrated to different sources on two devices. Both editions stay in your library
        until you choose one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notes edited on two devices:</strong> the later notes show for now. Choose <strong>Keep these</strong> for one version, or
        <strong>Keep both</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A deleted profile:</strong> a profile was deleted on one device while another device still changed things in it. It's kept
        until you choose <strong>Keep profile</strong> or delete it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="restore" title="Restore a backup while syncing">
    <p>
      Restoring a backup on a device that syncs asks <strong>How far the restore reaches</strong> (see
      <a href={sitePath("docs/backups-and-storage/")}>Backups and storage</a>):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Restore on this device</strong> fills in what's missing or older. Changes made on your other devices after the backup was made
        are kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Roll back the whole account</strong> returns every device to the backup. Libraries, progress, history and settings changed
        anywhere after it are lost; profiles the backup doesn't have, downloads and reading statistics stay.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>

  <Task id="status" title="What the status means">
    <p>
      Sync's status icon sits in the app bar of Library, Updates, History and Statistics, or at the foot of the side dock in a wide window,
      and opens the Sync page. When sync stops until you act, a message on the screen you're on says why. On the Sync page,
      <strong>Live</strong> after the account means changes from other devices arrive as they happen.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The Sync page says</th> <th>What to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Can't reach</strong> the server</td>
          <td>Check the connection and that the server is running. Changes wait on the device and nothing is lost.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>First download waits for Wi-Fi</strong></td>
          <td>Connect to Wi-Fi, or turn on <strong>Use mobile data for covers, fonts and first download</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Syncing paused</strong>, or <strong>Changes wait for Wi-Fi</strong></td>
          <td>Changes wait on the device. Turn off <strong>Pause syncing</strong>, or connect to Wi-Fi.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Live updates aren't working</strong></td>
          <td>
            Changes from other devices come when you open Katari and every few minutes, not as they happen. Whoever runs the server lets
            WebSockets through its proxy, as <a href={sitePath("docs/sync-server/#https")}>HTTPS behind a reverse proxy</a> describes.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Sync failed on this device</strong></td>
          <td>
            Something on the device stopped the sync, not the connection. <strong>Sync now</strong> tries again; if it keeps failing, update
            Katari, and share crash logs from <Path>Settings › Advanced</Path> to report it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The server <strong>needs a server update</strong></td>
          <td>
            This version of Katari needs a newer server. Whoever runs it follows
            <a href={sitePath("docs/sync-server/#update")}>Update the server</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong>Update Katari to keep syncing</strong>, <strong>Update Katari to sync</strong> something, or synced changes
            <strong>wait for a Katari update</strong>
          </td>
          <td>Another device or the server is newer than this app. Update Katari on this device.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Catching the server up</strong></td>
          <td>The server went back to an older copy, and this device is sending it what it's missing. Let it finish.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Your sync server was reset</strong></td>
          <td>
            The server has no accounts now. Once its admin account exists again, <strong>Set it up again</strong> signs in, and this device fills
            the server in.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>This device was removed</strong>, or the account was <strong>disabled</strong></td>
          <td>The device keeps everything and works on its own. <strong>Sign in again</strong> when you want it back.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Task>

  <Task id="remove" title="Remove a device or disconnect">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Under <strong>Devices</strong> on the Sync page, <strong>Remove</strong> another device. It stops syncing and keeps what it holds; it
        has to be linked again to rejoin.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Rename</strong> this device to change the name your other devices show.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Disconnect this device</strong> stops it syncing. Its library, history and settings stay, and your account and other devices
        carry on.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the server moved to a new address, enter it under <strong>Server address</strong>; the device stays signed in when the same server
        answers there.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Deleting your synced data or your account is done on the server's web page, under <strong>My account</strong>.
    </p>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
